{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1985 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "49af6982fc167b9647ed83092e4415ba19ac8ead817dd9d99613fc4b697c55cd" :edb/db-before-t 1984 :edb/db-after-t 1985 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535297 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:32:09.113Z" 13194139535297 true] [17592186092017 1100 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" 13194139535297 false] [17592186092017 1100 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/371521f0acd44811e109c10460e0cc99eb41d256f37f209dcefc003fd78ea70e.png)\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186092017 1145 "From the given information, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/c95b308a6f55de3acc8a2bf446b38ac10500dd506795898b0892952147d5c9e7.png)\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186092017 1145 "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535297 false] [17592186092018 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" 13194139535297 false] [17592186092018 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/7b8cdc56144f73cd1e3510c155508478767d0bf058e854e39f52bcad0f2e0413.png)\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186092018 1145 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/e7f7558d370d506a87ea9466703f9f9ac2e4ea970572b67c06d2114cdbc0f6fa.png)\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535297 true] [17592186092018 1145 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535297 false] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342358 13194139535297 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342372 13194139535297 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342393 13194139535297 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342398 13194139535297 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342365 13194139535297 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342379 13194139535297 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342386 13194139535297 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342404 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342359 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1089 "q-12271" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1100 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1113 #uuid "1593d047-1fe0-507f-9ebe-ac5fb8102d96" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342358 1145 "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-(-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342360 13194139535297 true] [17592186342359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342361 13194139535297 true] [17592186342359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342362 13194139535297 true] [17592186342359 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342363 13194139535297 true] [1