on of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,4),(-4,4),(12,4),$ and $(7,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(4,4)$" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342365 1113 #uuid "185591ef-5105-5530-b9a8-c36a84a0c979" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342365 1145 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,4),(-4,4),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535297 true] [17592186342366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342367 13194139535297 true] [17592186342366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342368 13194139535297 true] [17